TASK 3 – EXPLORATORY DATA ANALYSIS REPORT

Smart City Public Safety and Emergency Response

1. Introduction
The Smart City Public Safety and Emergency Response project focuses on analyzing public safety incidents and understanding the factors associated with different emergency levels.
Exploratory Data Analysis (EDA) was performed on the cleaned dataset to understand the structure, distribution, patterns, and relationships between the variables.
The analysis provides useful insights that can support public safety monitoring and future data-driven decision-making.

2. Objectives
The main objectives of this EDA are:
    To understand the structure and characteristics of the dataset.
    To analyze categorical and numerical variables.
    To study the distribution of emergency levels.
    To identify common incident types and affected areas.
    To analyze relationships between incident characteristics and emergency levels.
    To study severity scores and emergency response times.
    To identify relationships between numerical variables using correlation analysis.
    To extract meaningful patterns from the dataset.

3. Dataset Description
The dataset contains records related to Smart City public safety incidents.

Important features include:
    Incident_ID – Unique identifier for each incident.  
    Incident_Type – Type of public safety incident.
    Area – Location where the incident occurred.
    Time_of_Day – Time category of the incident.
    Day_of_Week – Day on which the incident occurred.
    Weather – Weather condition during the incident.
    Crowd_Level – Crowd condition at the incident location.
    CCTV_Available – Availability of CCTV.
    Police_Response_Time – Police response time.
    Ambulance_Response_Time – Ambulance response time.
    Fire_Response_Time – Fire service response time.
    Number_of_People – Number of people involved or affected.
    Severity_Score – Severity level represented numerically.
    Previous_Incidents – Number of previous incidents.
    Emergency_Level – Emergency classification of the incident.
The cleaned dataset contains 9,800 records and 15 columns.

4. Data Quality Verification
The cleaned dataset was used for EDA after the data-cleaning stage.

The dataset was checked for:
    Missing values
    Duplicate records
    Data types
    Numerical statistics
    Categorical distributions

The cleaned dataset contains no missing values and no duplicate records, making it suitable for exploratory analysis.

5. Exploratory Data Analysis
5.1 Emergency Level Distribution
The distribution of the Emergency_Level variable was analyzed using a count plot.
The emergency levels are:
    Low
    Medium
    High

The analysis shows that High emergency incidents form the largest category, followed by Medium and Low incidents.
This indicates that the dataset is imbalanced toward high-emergency incidents and this distribution should be considered when interpreting analysis and predictive modeling results.

5.2 Incident Type Distribution
    The frequency of different incident types was analyzed.
    This visualization helps identify which types of public safety incidents occur more frequently.
    Understanding the distribution of incident types can help authorities prioritize monitoring and allocate emergency resources more effectively.

5.3 Incidents by Area
    The number of incidents in different areas was analyzed.
    The area-wise distribution helps identify locations where incidents occur more frequently.

This information can be useful for planning:
    Police deployment
    Emergency response resources
    CCTV monitoring
    Public safety measures

5.4 Emergency Level by Incident Type
    The relationship between Incident_Type and Emergency_Level was analyzed.
    The comparison shows how different incident categories are distributed across Low, Medium, and High emergency levels.
    This analysis helps identify incident categories that are more frequently associated with serious emergency situations.

5.5 Emergency Level by Area
    Emergency levels were compared across different areas.
    This analysis helps identify whether certain locations contain a higher proportion of serious incidents.
    Area-based emergency analysis can support better resource allocation and location-specific safety planning.

5.6 Weather vs Emergency Level
    The relationship between weather conditions and emergency levels was analyzed.
    Different weather categories were compared with Low, Medium, and High emergency incidents.
    This analysis helps determine whether particular weather conditions are associated with changes in the occurrence or severity of public safety incidents.

5.7 Crowd Level vs Emergency Level
    Crowd level was compared with emergency level.
    The analysis helps understand whether incidents occurring under higher crowd conditions are associated with different emergency-level patterns.
    This can be useful for crowd management and public-event safety planning.

5.8 Time of Day vs Emergency Level
    Emergency levels were analyzed across different times of the day.
    This helps identify possible time-based patterns in public safety incidents.
    Such patterns can support better scheduling of emergency personnel and monitoring resources.

6. Numerical Feature Analysis

The following numerical variables were analyzed:
    Police Response Time
    Ambulance Response Time
    Fire Response Time
    Number of People
    Severity Score
    Previous Incidents
Histograms were used to understand the distribution of these variables.
Box plots were also used to compare response times and severity scores across different emergency levels.

7. Severity Score Analysis
    The Severity_Score was analyzed using box plots across the three emergency levels.
    Severity score provides a numerical representation of incident seriousness.
    Comparing severity scores across emergency categories helps determine whether higher emergency levels are associated with higher severity values.
    This also provides a useful foundation for predictive modeling.

8. Response Time Analysis
    Police, ambulance, and fire response times were analyzed separately.
    Box plots were used to compare response-time distributions across emergency levels.
    This analysis provides an understanding of emergency-service response patterns and can help identify differences in response times between incident categories.

9. Correlation Analysis
    A correlation heatmap was generated for the numerical variables.

The analysis included:
    Police Response Time
    Ambulance Response Time
    Fire Response Time
    Number of People
    Severity Score
    Previous Incidents

Correlation analysis helps identify the strength and direction of relationships between numerical variables.
Strong correlations can indicate variables that may provide useful information for future predictive analysis, while weak correlations indicate limited linear relationships.

10. Key Findings

The major findings from the EDA are:
    The dataset contains 9,800 cleaned incident records.
    The Emergency_Level distribution is dominated by High emergency incidents.
    Incident types are not equally distributed, allowing identification of frequently occurring incident categories.
    Incident frequency varies across different areas.
    Emergency levels can be compared with incident type, area, weather, crowd level, and time of day to identify useful patterns.
    Severity scores provide an important numerical indicator of incident seriousness.
    Police, ambulance, and fire response times show different distributions across emergency levels.
    Correlation analysis provides information about relationships between numerical variables.
    The EDA results provide a useful foundation for predictive modeling and future public safety analysis.

11. Tools and Technologies

The following tools and technologies were used:
    Python
    Jupyter Notebook
    Pandas
    NumPy
    Matplotlib
    Seaborn

These tools were used for data loading, statistical analysis, data exploration, and visualization.

12. Conclusion
Exploratory Data Analysis was successfully performed on the Smart City Public Safety dataset.
The analysis examined the dataset structure, emergency-level distribution, incident types, areas, weather conditions, crowd levels, time of day, severity scores, response times, and numerical correlations.
The visualizations helped identify patterns and relationships within the public safety data. These findings can support better understanding of emergency incidents and provide a foundation for future machine learning and data-driven public safety solutions.
The EDA stage also complements the project's ML component by providing a better understanding of the variables before applying predictive models. The overall problem statement specifically positions the project as a progression from data preprocessing and traditional ML toward more advanced public-safety capabilities.